To achieve this, we will create a Terraform configuration that sets up an AWS Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) and a Route 53 DNS record pointing to it. The configuration will include:

1. An AWS provider configuration specifying the region.
2. A Route 53 hosted zone named "primary".
3. An Elastic Load Balancer named "main".
4. A Route 53 A record that points to the DNS name of the ELB.

We'll also create an IAM role for the ELB with the necessary permissions, although for simplicity, we'll focus on the ELB creation without detailed target group and listener configurations. The IAM role will have a basic policy attached to allow logging to CloudWatch.

Here's the complete Terraform HCL configuration:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"  # You can change this to your preferred region
}

# Create an IAM role for the ELB
resource "aws_iam_role" "elb_service_role" {
  name = "elb-service-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "elasticloadbalancing.amazonaws.com"
        }
      },
    ]
  })
}

# Attach a policy to the IAM role for logging to CloudWatch
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "elb_logging_policy" {
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AmazonElasticLoadBalancingFullAccess"
  role       = aws_iam_role.elb_service_role.name
}

# Create a Route 53 hosted zone
resource "aws_route53_zone" "primary" {
  name = "example.com"  # Replace with your domain name
}

# Create an Elastic Load Balancer
resource "aws_lb" "main" {
  name               = "main"
  internal           = false
  security_groups    = [aws_security_group.lb_sg.id]
  subnets            = ["subnet-12345678", "subnet-87654321"]  # Replace with your subnet IDs
  enable_deletion_protection = true

  tags = {
    Name = "main"
  }
}

# Create a security group for the ELB
resource "aws_security_group" "lb_sg" {
  name        = "lb-sg"
  description = "Security group for ELB"
  vpc_id      = "vpc-12345678"  # Replace with your VPC ID

  ingress {
    from_port   = 80
    to_port     = 80
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

# Create a Route 53 A record pointing to the ELB
resource "aws_route53_record" "elb_dns" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.primary.zone_id
  name    = "www.example.com"  # Replace with your desired subdomain
  type    = "A"

  alias {
    name                   = aws_lb.main.dns_name
    zone_id                = aws_lb.main.zone_id
    evaluate_target_health = true
  }
}
```

### Notes:
- Replace `"example.com"` with your actual domain name.
- Replace `["subnet-12345678", "subnet-87654321"]` with your actual subnet IDs.
- Replace `"vpc-12345678"` with your actual VPC ID.
- The security group allows HTTP traffic on port 80 from any IP address. Adjust the rules as needed for your use case.
- The IAM role and policy attachment are set up for basic ELB functionality, including logging to CloudWatch. For more advanced configurations, additional policies and settings may be required.

This configuration is deployable with Terraform version `~> 1.9.8` and the AWS provider version `~> 5.75`.

