To create a SageMaker Domain resource using Terraform, we need to set up the necessary AWS provider configuration, define the required IAM roles, and then create the SageMaker Domain resource itself. The SageMaker Domain requires an execution role and a Jupyter server app settings role. Below is the complete Terraform configuration to achieve this:

### Description:
- **Provider Configuration**: Configures the AWS provider with a specific region.
- **IAM Roles**: Creates two IAM roles:
  - `sagemaker_execution_role`: This role is used by SageMaker to execute training jobs, processing jobs, and other tasks.
  - `jupyter_server_app_role`: This role is used by the Jupyter server app within the SageMaker domain.
- **SageMaker Domain**: Creates a SageMaker domain with the specified IAM roles.

### Terraform HCL Configuration:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"  # You can change this to your preferred region
}

# IAM Role for SageMaker Execution
resource "aws_iam_role" "sagemaker_execution_role" {
  name = "sagemaker-execution-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "sagemaker.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}

# IAM Policy for SageMaker Execution Role
resource "aws_iam_policy" "sagemaker_execution_policy" {
  name        = "sagemaker-execution-policy"
  description = "Policy for SageMaker execution role"

  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = [
          "s3:GetObject",
          "s3:PutObject",
          "s3:ListBucket"
        ]
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Resource = "*"
      },
      {
        Action = [
          "logs:CreateLogGroup",
          "logs:CreateLogStream",
          "logs:PutLogEvents"
        ]
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}

# Attach the policy to the SageMaker Execution Role
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "sagemaker_execution_policy_attachment" {
  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.sagemaker_execution_policy.arn
  role       = aws_iam_role.sagemaker_execution_role.name
}

# IAM Role for Jupyter Server App
resource "aws_iam_role" "jupyter_server_app_role" {
  name = "jupyter-server-app-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "sagemaker.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}

# IAM Policy for Jupyter Server App Role
resource "aws_iam_policy" "jupyter_server_app_policy" {
  name        = "jupyter-server-app-policy"
  description = "Policy for Jupyter server app role"

  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = [
          "s3:GetObject",
          "s3:PutObject",
          "s3:ListBucket"
        ]
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Resource = "*"
      },
      {
        Action = [
          "logs:CreateLogGroup",
          "logs:CreateLogStream",
          "logs:PutLogEvents"
        ]
        Effect   = "Allow"
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}

# Attach the policy to the Jupyter Server App Role
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "jupyter_server_app_policy_attachment" {
  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.jupyter_server_app_policy.arn
  role       = aws_iam_role.jupyter_server_app_role.name
}

# SageMaker Domain
resource "aws_sagemaker_domain" "example_domain" {
  domain_name         = "example-sagemaker-domain"
  auth_mode           = "IAM"
  vpc_id              = "vpc-12345678"  # Replace with your VPC ID
  subnet_ids          = ["subnet-12345678", "subnet-87654321"]  # Replace with your subnet IDs
  default_user_settings {
    jupyter_server_app_settings {
      default_resource_spec {
        instance_type = "ml.t3.medium"
      }
    }
  }
  execution_role_arn  = aws_iam_role.sagemaker_execution_role.arn
}
```

### Notes:
- Replace `"vpc-12345678"` and `["subnet-12345678", "subnet-87654321"]` with your actual VPC ID and subnet IDs.
- The policies attached to the roles are basic and may need to be adjusted based on your specific requirements and security policies.
- Ensure that the region specified in the provider block matches the region where your VPC and subnets are located.

